  <h1>India’s Semiconductor Mission — Building Tech Sovereignty</h1>
  <div><em>Economic Times | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Semiconductors are the backbone of the digital economy, powering everything from smartphones to defence systems. India, heavily dependent on imports (especially from East Asia), launched the India Semiconductor Mission (ISM) in 2021 to build domestic capacity. In 2024, Economic Times highlighted both progress and hurdles in India’s journey toward tech sovereignty.
________________________________________
Why Semiconductors Matter
•	Strategic Sector: Used in defence, telecom, automobiles, AI, and space.
•	Global Crisis: COVID-19 and geopolitical tensions (US–China tech war) exposed vulnerabilities.
•	Economic Impact: Semiconductor market in India expected to cross $64 billion by 2026.
________________________________________
India’s Semiconductor Mission
1.	Scheme: ₹76,000 crore package to support fabs, assembly, testing, and design.
2.	Key Projects:
o	Micron’s $2.7 billion ATMP (Assembly, Testing, Marking, Packaging) unit in Gujarat (2023).
o	Vedanta–Foxconn proposal (facing delays).
o	ISMC consortium exploring fab in Karnataka.
3.	Design Ecosystem: Support for chip design startups through C-DAC and MeitY.
________________________________________
Progress in 2024
•	Micron facility construction underway; expected production by 2025.
•	30+ design startups supported under Design Linked Incentive (DLI) scheme.
•	Global partnerships with US, Japan, and Taiwan.
•	States like Gujarat and Karnataka emerging as semiconductor hubs.
________________________________________
Challenges
1.	Capital Intensity: Fabs require ~$10 billion+ investment per unit.
2.	Skill Gaps: Shortage of semiconductor engineers and technicians.
3.	Infrastructure: High energy and water requirements strain local ecosystems.
4.	Global Competition: Established hubs (Taiwan, South Korea) far ahead.
5.	Policy Uncertainty: Delays in approvals and clarity on long-term subsidies.
________________________________________
Strategic Significance
•	National Security: Reduces vulnerability to supply shocks and foreign dependence.
•	Economic Growth: Boosts “Make in India” and exports.
•	Global Alliances: Positions India in Quad-led tech partnerships.
•	Employment: Potential to create 1 million direct and indirect jobs.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-III (Economy & Tech): Industrial policy, digital economy.
•	GS-II (IR): Technology diplomacy, strategic autonomy.
•	Essay:
o	“Technology is the new currency of power.”
o	“Building self-reliance in critical sectors is the essence of sovereignty.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Tech rivalry in global politics; India’s quest for autonomy.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Focus on ATMP & Design: Build strength in chip design and packaging before fabs.<br/>2.	Skill Development: Specialized semiconductor courses in IITs, IIITs.<br/>3.	Policy Stability: Long-term subsidies and tax breaks to attract investors.<br/>4.	Sustainable Infrastructure: Ensure renewable energy and water recycling.<br/>5.	Global Partnerships: Deepen ties with Taiwan, US, and Japan for tech transfer.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s semiconductor mission is a bold attempt to secure tech sovereignty in a critical sector. While challenges of scale, cost, and skills remain, steady progress in design and packaging could make India a key player in global supply chains. For UPSC aspirants, this case reflects how industrial policy, security, and foreign policy converge in technology governance.</p>
